"CONSTRAINT VERBALIZATION AS CANON (the generator's sentences). engine/rust built these three sentences with format! inside generator_cells_native and tools/rust-station had no way to say them at all, which is the gap: the words a constraint is READ ALOUD in are ORM's own surface syntax, not a host's string handling. metamodel/core.md is WRITTEN in them -- Each Object Type has at most one Plural -- so verbalization is exactly the emit direction of the language system:reading_parse reads, and a host that spells it privately is holding meaning. The uniqueness and mandatory arms fire only with two or more players, matching the generator's own guard: a unary fact type has no second player to name and is passed over rather than bottomed. A deontic constraint of any flavour verbalizes as its own identifier, which is what the generator does with every kind whose name starts deontic; the prefix test is strip_prefix, whose policy-free tail-or-s answer (resolution.md, registered) makes starts-with expressible without a second primitive -- the stripped text differs from the original exactly when the prefix was there. Sentences are assembled with implode, the same registered base prim main:report_text is built from, so this needs nothing new.",

"THE CONSTRAINT VERBALIZATION AS THE GENERATOR ASKS FOR IT: <tag, sentence>. system:con_text answers the sentence alone, which is the half the hosts had in common; the generator also needs the KIND TAG it files the sentence under, and building the sentence without the tag left the branch in the host anyway. Uniqueness files as UC and mandatory as MC. A deontic constraint files as UC TOO, which is the row that looks like a mistake and is not: the tag says how the generator groups the sentence, not what the constraint is, and a deontic obligation groups with the uniqueness family there. This composes con_text rather than restating the sentences, so the two cannot drift apart.",

"THE dsl CELL ROW FOR A NOUN, and every component of it SORTED. The row is <noun, kind, readings, constraints, transitions> and the sorting is the meaning rather than tidiness: engine.py says so in its own comment, that the components project SETS and store emission order must not leak into the row bytes -- it differed across hosts on rule-chain apps and embedded the constraint cell row order into the dsl rows. Three components, three sorts, because the shapes differ: readings are flat names and sort by rmap:sortnames, constraints are <tag, sentence> pairs and transitions <trigger, from, to> triples, both of which system:sort_asc orders by first element. The transitions DEDUPE first: a governed noun sees the union of every machine triple, so the same triple arrives more than once, and the set is what the row means.",

"EVERY DECLARED TYPE WITH ITS KIND, sorted. The generator labels an ObjectType entity and a ValueType value, and asks for them in name order both times it reads them. Composed from the two halves canon already had -- system:ev_entities answers the object types and system:ctx_vals the value types -- rather than scanning instanceOf again, so a third kind appearing in that population is excluded here by the same rule that excludes it there. The LABELS are the generator vocabulary, not the metamodel: instanceOf says ObjectType and ValueType, the dsl rows say entity and value, and this def is where that translation lives instead of in a conditional expression the hosts each spell for themselves.",

