"KAHN PARENTS-FIRST, the last of op_sql_project's text-independent work (engine/rust 11011, the old engine's rmap.rs Phase 3). A table is ready when every parent it references is already placed, is itself (self-reference), or is not a projected table at all (external). Ready tables go out sorted by name; when NO table is ready the remainder is a cycle and goes out sorted, which terminates. The chain family (rmap:chainof/chainroots/pidchains) does NOT serve here -- those are reference-scheme identification chains, a different graph -- so this is new canon rather than a composition of what was there. Recursion by WHILE over <order, remaining, allnames>: the wave structure IS the fixpoint, and it is what makes the sort stable per wave rather than global.",
"COLUMN NAME DISAMBIGUATION, which engine/rust carries TWICE (the entity_columns closure and the own-fact-type loop both keep a `seen` map and suffix _2, _3): first occurrence of a base keeps it, the Nth gets _N. One naming pass is what keeps the DDL and the rows agreeing, so it being written twice is exactly the drift worth removing. Not in canon under any name -- rmap:sepnames is NORMA-comparison naming and rmap:valuecolname appends Value, neither is this. WHILE rather than INSERT because the count is over what came BEFORE, and INSERT folds from the right.",
"rmap:colsof is a table's absorbed columns IN COLUMN ORDER, read off the materialized rmapColumns rows <table,col,ft>: restrict to the table, order by the column number, project the fact type. Codd 1972's algebra doing exactly what engine/rust does by hand with a HashMap and a sort (main.rs 10725). The rows are reshaped to <col,ft> BEFORE sorting because system:ins_asc compares through element 1 -- sorting the raw <table,col,ft> row would order by the table, which is constant here, and leave the columns in whatever order the cell happened to hold. The column ORDER is load-bearing: it is what makes the DDL's columns and the rows' values line up.",
"rmap:rolesof is rmap:colsof's twin over the materialized `role` rows <_,ft,pos,player>: restrict to the fact type, order by position, keep <pos,player>. NOT rmap:ftplayers -- that derives players from rmap:mi/vtcts at MODEL level, while this reads the cell a compile already wrote, which is the same information at a later stage. engine/rust sorted the (pos,player) TUPLE lexicographically; system:ins_asc compares through element 1 only, so this orders by position alone. Those agree because a position is the role's index WITHIN its fact type and so is unique there -- the tie case below pins what canon does anyway rather than leaving it assumed.",
"THE REFERENCE MODE and the key column it names. engine/rust builds one map from two cells with OPPOSITE precedence (main.rs): refScheme rows are inserted, so the LAST row for a name wins; refMode rows are setdefault, so the FIRST row wins and only where refScheme said nothing; absent from both is 'id'. That asymmetry is the whole content -- a version that took first from both, or last from both, would pass any case that did not present a name twice, so the cases below present each name twice on purpose. Note 1r earns its keep here: last-of-sequence is a base primitive, and this is the reason it exists.",
"rmap:entities: the declared object types, restricted off the instanceOf rows <name,kind> where the kind is ObjectType. The LENGTH GUARD is not defensive noise -- engine/rust tests it.len() >= 2 and skips, and without the same test here 2 of a one-element row is bottom, eq of bottom is bottom, and COND on bottom makes the WHOLE sweep bottom rather than skipping one row (13.2 again). No dedup: the host holds these in a set and order is irrelevant, so a repeated name costs nothing and dedup would only invite the last-occurrence surprise.",
"rmap:sortnames sorts BARE ATOMS, which system:sort_asc cannot: ins_asc compares through element 1, so a bare name answers bottom (1 of an atom). Wrap each name as a 1-sequence, sort, unwrap. rmap:ord_names dodged this by sorting the tables it already had; this is the general fix, and it is what engine/rust's own.sort() on Vec<String> was doing.",
"THE ENTITY-ID SWEEP's two pure halves. rmap:playerpos answers which fact types name a table and at WHICH role position -- engine/rust's nested loop over role_ft_order filtering `player != table`. rmap:atpos projects that position out of a population's rows. Note how the position is applied: in FP a positive integer IS a selector function, so apply:<pos,row> selects dynamically (Backus 11.2.1) -- no host indexing arithmetic, and no new primitive. The length guard is the same one rmap:ent_keep needs, for the same reason: a row shorter than the position would answer bottom and take the whole projection with it, where engine/rust's `it.len() >= p` merely skips the row.",
