"THE PLAYERS OF A FACT TYPE, IN ROLE ORDER. Answers <position, player> pairs sorted by position, which is the order every caller wanted and each one sorted for itself -- the hosts built a map from fact type to unsorted pairs and then wrote sorted(...) at each read site, so the ordering was restated at every use and could be forgotten at one. It is the def order here. The sort is by POSITION and not by player: role 1 of P_manages_P and role 2 are both Person, so sorting by name would leave the manager and the managed in whichever order the role rows happened to be stored, and the reading template fills its slots by position.",

"THE ARITY OF A FACT TYPE -- its highest role position. Built on system:ft_roles, which already answers the pairs sorted by position, so the arity is the last one and no second sort is needed. A fact type with NO role rows answers TWO, not zero and not one, and that default is the meaning rather than a convenience: the caller asks this to decide whether a fact type is UNARY, and a fact type whose roles have not been read yet must not look unary. Answering 0 or phi would make an unread fact type compile as a unary one, which routes its rows through a different shape entirely.",

"WHERE AN ABSORBED FACT TYPE SITS IN ITS WIDE ROW. The 3NF row is <key, v1, v2, ...>, so the key holds position 1 and the first absorbed column position 2 -- which is why this answers the column index PLUS ONE and not the index itself. system:table_columns already answers the absorbed fact types in declaration order, and it is CURRIED: applied to the table, then to the partition pairs, which is how the host binding calls it and not something a single pair operand reaches. A fact type absorbed into no column answers bottom rather than a position: writing its value at a guessed column would overwrite whichever fact type actually lives there.",

"THE SUBTYPE LATTICE AROUND A NOUN, both directions. A subtype row is <subtype, supertype>, so the two questions read OPPOSITE columns and neither is the other reversed: the supertypes of X are the second column of rows whose FIRST is X, and the subtypes of X are the first column of rows whose SECOND is X. They are separate defs for that reason -- one def with a direction flag would let a caller pass the wrong flag and get a plausible answer, since both answer noun names. Both are deduped and sorted by name: a lattice is a set, and the order is asked for rather than inherited from the row order.",

"EVERY VALUE A KEY HOLDS IN A BINARY POPULATION, deduped and sorted. Distinct from csdp:lookup and rmap:lookup0, which both answer the FIRST match only -- one as a scalar and one as a one-tuple -- and that difference is the reason this exists rather than reusing either: a noun is an instance of more than one thing at once, so a first-match lookup would report an object type and silently drop the rest of what it is. Sorted and deduped because the answer is a SET of what the key is, not a history of how the rows were written.",

"WHAT THE GOVERNEDBY CLOSURE IS MADE OF. A noun is governed by the machine it is bound to, and by any machine governing a SUPERTYPE -- two rules, and which two was host code in engine.py governance_rules. It is data here: rows of <rule name, populations read, head projection, fact type derived>, so extending the closure is a ROW rather than an edit to a host function, the same move as the compile dispatch in system:h_table. The DERIVED fact type rides along because the host otherwise states it a third time: once in the plan, once in the ruleReads tuple it appends, once in the ruleDerives tuple. Three statements of one fact, free to disagree. The BASE rule reads smDef and projects <2,1>, turning <machine, noun> into <noun, machine>; the STEP rule reads subtype beside the closure being built and projects <1,3>, which is what makes it recursive and what test_pure_recursion_is_admitted asserts must stay admissible. The order matters: base before step, so the first round has something to close over.",

