"THE SECOND, and the other one Backus writes out (13.3.2): `Def rhoCONS = alpha apply o tl o distr. This definition results in <CONS,f1,...,fn> -- where the fi are objects -- representing the same function as [rho f1, ..., rho fn]'. Read it on <<CONS,f1..fn>, x>: distr pairs every element of the form with the operand, giving <<CONS,x>, <f1,x>, ..., <fn,x>>; tl drops the controlling operator's own pair; alpha apply reduces each <fi,x> to fi:x. The result is <f1:x, ..., fn:x>, which is construction. 8346 sites in canon, so with CONST that is 14083 form applications a session now resolving through canon rather than through eight hand-written copies. Note what this DEF is built from: ALPHA, tl, distr, apply, COMP -- all base. Construction is a THEOREM of the base, not a member of it, and the same is true of everything the base audit reduced (sl:slug is the worked example, four DEFs over chars/INSERT/implode). The hosts keep their versions as certified-equal overrides -- head.part.js's FASTPRIMS says the rule already: `the DEF stays the meaning; the head evaluates its extensional equal; the wall certifies identity'.",

