"THE CANON RENDERER (the prerequisite for a station evaluating the shared case table): system:show renders ANY value as text. Four engine hosts each carry a PRIVATE show() -- thin.py and its twins in engine/java, engine/csharp, engine/rust -- and the four stations carry none at all, which is exactly why no station could ever run the case table: the host contract is six lines that print out[0], so the text has to be built HERE. A renderer is a function, not a law, so canon is where it belongs, by the same argument that moved the base into the kernels. The int/str split is the whole difficulty, since a renderer must print 42 and the STRING 42 differently while the atom domain holds both: eq does NOT coerce (the case table has pinned eq<1,1-as-text> = F since long before this) and implode stringifies any atom, so isnum(x) <=> not eq<x, implode<empty, <x>>> is total with no new primitive. Empty sequence answers the empty parens and a one-sequence answers a single parenthesised item, transcribing the renderer python already runs rather than inventing a convention. BOTTOM is deliberately absent: bottom is the mu refusing, not a value, and every f(bottom) is bottom, so NO canon def can branch on it -- which is why the table must be driven one case per invocation, where a station own refusal shows at the boundary and the driver compares it.",

