,
"THE TWO CONSTRAINT FAMILIES. AREST.tex:148 gives compile's algorithm for a constraint in one sentence: every constraint of Definition 4 compiles to a restriction whose predicate falls in one of two families -- a CARDINALITY COUNT against declared bounds, or a MEMBERSHIP TEST against a target population -- with POLARITY the only parameter separating symmetric from asymmetric and subset from exclusion. Definition 4 lists ten kinds; these are the two sets they fall into. Written as data because a convention that is only ever computed cannot be checked, cannot take an exception, and cannot be read by anything that does not already know it -- and because this exact split was 7,196 lines of host code in two languages, which is what a computed convention costs.",
DEF("compile:count_kinds",
    K(S3(A("uniqueness"), A("frequency"), A("cardinality")))),

DEF("compile:member_kinds",
    K(S7(A("mandatory"), A("ring"), A("value_comparison"), A("subset"), A("equality"), A("exclusion"), A("value")))),

"WHICH FAMILY A CONSTRAINT KIND COMPILES INTO. A kind in neither set is not a constraint this fragment admits, and the empty answer is the refusal -- Definition 5 rejects what the grammar does not generate, so a kind with no family must not silently acquire one.",
DEF("compile:family",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:count_kinds"))),
       K(A("count")),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:member_kinds"))),
          K(A("member")),
          K(PHI())))),

"POLARITY, the one parameter the two families take. AREST.tex:148 names the two axes it separates: symmetric from asymmetric, and subset from exclusion. A subset constraint requires its rows to be IN the target population and an exclusion requires them to be OUT, and that is the whole difference between the two objects -- so the polarity is a value here rather than a second builder.",
DEF("compile:positive_kinds",
    K(S4(A("mandatory"), A("subset"), A("equality"), A("value")))),

DEF("compile:polarity",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:positive_kinds"))),
       K(A("in")),
       K(A("out")))),

"WHICH OBJECT EACH CONSTRAINT KIND COMPILES TO. compile:family says which of the paper's two predicate families a kind falls in; this says which canon object actually builds its restriction. system:cs_builders set the precedent for eight set-comparison specs, with a note conceding that the OPERAND SHAPES stayed host -- and the hosts are gone, so the mapping lives here or nowhere. THREE VOCABULARIES DISAGREE, which is why this table is keyed on what is STORED rather than on any one of them. Definition 4 names ten kinds including value-comparison and exclusion; metamodel/core.md:382 enumerates ten Constraint Type Family values with set-comparison and deontic in their place; and the corpus's own constraint cells carry fourteen, including four that none of the lists mention. Measured over every compiled store: 5,664 constraints, of which uniqueness and mandatory alone are 4,302. The last three rows here are the VARIANTS -- spanning uniqueness is a uniqueness restriction over a wider column set, disjunctive mandatory is ORM's inclusive-or, and exclusive_or already had its object -- so they were refusals with builders sitting right beside them.",
DEF("compile:builders_direct",
    K(S8(S2(A("uniqueness"), A("constraints:uniqueness")), S2(A("frequency"), A("constraints:frequency")), S2(A("mandatory"), A("constraints:mandatory")), S2(A("subset"), A("constraints:subset")), S2(A("equality"), A("constraints:equality")), S2(A("exclusion"), A("constraints:exclusion")), S2(A("value_comparison"), A("constraints:value_comparison")), S2(A("cardinality"), A("constraints:cardinality"))))),

DEF("compile:builders_variant",
    K(S4(S2(A("spanning_uniqueness"), A("constraints:uniqueness")),
         S2(A("disjunctive_mandatory"), A("constraints:inclusive_or")),
         S2(A("exclusive_or"), A("constraints:exclusive_or")),
         S2(A("deontic_forbidden"), A("constraints:deontic_forbidden"))))),

"MODALITY IS AN ATTRIBUTE, NOT A KIND, AND ALSO A DIFFERENT OBJECT. Definition 4 says each constraint is alethic OR deontic -- one constraint carrying a modality -- while metamodel/core.md:382 lists 'deontic' among the ten families and the store records deontic_obligatory and deontic_forbidden as kinds, 439 of them. Both readings are defensible and this keeps them apart rather than picking one: the MODALITY is read off the tag here, and the tag still names a builder in the variant table, because a deontic restriction really is a different object -- system:validate_modal reports a deontic violation and COMMITS where an alethic one blocks, so they cannot share one. What the store calls a kind is a kind-plus-modality, and only the second half of that was ever missing.",
DEF("compile:deontic_tags",
    K(S2(A("deontic_obligatory"), A("deontic_forbidden")))),

DEF("compile:modality_of_tag",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:deontic_tags"))),
       K(A("deontic")),
       K(A("alethic")))),

"MODALITY OVER THE WHOLE TAG SEQUENCE, because it is not always the tag. read:parse marks a deontic subset as <subset, deontic> -- the KIND leads and the modality rides second -- while a deontic mandatory is the single tag deontic_obligatory. compile:modality_of_tag reads only the first element and therefore called that subset alethic, which is wrong in the direction that matters: an alethic constraint BLOCKS a commit where a deontic one reports and commits. Found by running compile:statement over read:witness_subset rather than over keys chosen to match the code.",
DEF("compile:modality_of",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:or2"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("deontic")), A("id"))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
                S3(A("CONS"), N(1), A("compile:deontic_tags"))))),
       K(A("deontic")),
       K(A("alethic")))),

"THE TWO KINDS THAT FAN OUT, and they fan out differently. Ring's sub-kinds are NAMED -- read:ring_kinds is the vocabulary, six of them, and every one has a constraints:ring_<kind> object -- so the builder is a name COMPUTED by prefixing, not a table row, which is what read:ring_tag already does one prefix short. Value's two are told apart by the SHAPE of the spec instead: a list of admitted values against a low/high pair, which is why they need a table where ring needs none. This row said `ring x11` when it held arities as a placeholder; eleven counted the constraints:ring_* DEFs, five of which are machinery (swapin, neq, wrap, chains, closure) rather than kinds.",
DEF("compile:builders_fanned",
    K(S2(A("ring"), A("value")))),

"THE RING TAGS AS read:parse EMITS THEM. The reader does not hand over <ring, acyclic>; read:ring_tag has already imploded the pair into the single atom ring_acyclic, and slot [2] of a parse carries that. Canon cannot take it apart again -- read:isprefix, read:firstn and read:dropn are all SEQUENCE operations and there is no explode -- so the two sides meet on the tag rather than on the pair, and the six tags are computed from read:ring_kinds by the same prefixing the reader used. This DEF first took the bare sub-kind, which read as the tidier contract right up until the probe showed the consumer could not produce one.",
DEF("compile:ring_tags",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S2(A("ALPHA"), S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"),
                         S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("ring_")), A("id"))))),
       A("read:ring_kinds"))),

DEF("compile:ring_builder",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:ring_tags"))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"),
          S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:")), A("id")))),
       K(PHI()))),

DEF("compile:value_builders",
    K(S2(S2(A("enumeration"), A("constraints:value_enumeration")),
         S2(A("range"), A("constraints:value_range"))))),

"WHICH OF DEFINITION 5's FAMILIES A PARSED TAG BELONGS TO. Definition 5 admits five sentence families and compile dispatches on them, so the families are rows rather than a COND nobody can read. Two things this makes visible that a dispatch would hide. The corpus stores `subtype` as a constraint kind 367 times and Definition 4 does not list it -- it is a DECLARATION, and routing it here is what stops it being counted as a constraint that refuses. And the fifth family, state-machine readings, has NO tag anywhere in canon: read: emits nothing for it, so compile cannot dispatch what parse never marks, and the row is absent rather than empty to say the gap is upstream.",
DEF("compile:family_declaration",
    K(S1(A("subtype")))),

DEF("compile:family_derivation",
    K(S2(A("derivation_rule"), A("derivation")))),

DEF("compile:family_population",
    K(S1(A("population")))),

"THE READER'S TAG AGAINST DEFINITION 4's KIND NAME, where the two spell it differently. read:parse tags a value constraint `values` while Definition 4 and the store both call the kind `value`, so the tag reaches no builder without this row. One row today, and it is a row rather than a special case in the lookup because the next disagreement between the reader's vocabulary and the paper's belongs beside it, not in another COND.",
DEF("compile:tag_kinds",
    K(S1(S2(A("values"), A("value"))))),

DEF("compile:kind_of_tag",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:tag_kinds")))),
       A("id"),
       S3(A("COMP"), N(2),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:tag_kinds")))))),

DEF("compile:family_of_tag",
    S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:family_declaration"))),
       K(A("declaration")),
       S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:family_derivation"))),
          K(A("derivation")),
          S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:family_population"))),
             K(A("population")),
             S4(A("COND"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:builder_of"),
                      S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:kind_of_tag"), A("id")), K(PHI())))),
                K(PHI()),
                K(A("constraint"))))))),

"COMPILE, ON ONE PARSED STATEMENT. read:parse's slot [2] carries <ftid, players, <tag, ...payload>> and this answers <ftid, family, kind, modality, builder> -- a ROW, which is why compile:model can simply collect them rather than thread an accumulator. AREST.tex:158 says compilation is functional on the resulting syntax objects, and a function whose answer is data is the whole of what that buys: the compiled statement can be inspected, counted, and diffed against the corpus without running anything. The refusals ride in the row too. A tag with no family answers the empty sequence in that column rather than failing the statement, so a store can be measured for what compile does NOT yet reach -- which is how the subtype misfiling and both remaining data gaps were found.",
DEF("compile:statement",
    S6(A("CONS"),
       N(1),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:family_of_tag"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:kind_of_tag"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:modality_of"), N(3)),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:builder_of"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:kind_of_tag"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
             K(PHI()))))),

"COMPILE, ON A WHOLE PARSE. read:parse answers three slots -- every sentence as <ftid, players>, then the READINGS as <ftid, players, uniqueness-spans, mandatory-spans, compact>, then the TAGGED records -- and compile:model answers the two halves that carry meaning: the readings, which declare fact types and their attached spans, and the compiled statements, one row each. AREST.tex:72 is the pipeline (R -> parse -> compile -> S subset of DEFS) and :329 makes the store step ordinary: the addressed entity is DEFS and the operation is compile . parse. Slot [0] is not carried: it is every sentence undifferentiated, which slots [1] and [2] already partition, so folding it in would double-count every statement.",
DEF("compile:statements",
    S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("compile:statement")), N(3))),

"THE ROLE ROWS OF A READING, which is the first place compile output becomes a DEFS CELL rather than a shape of its own. The store's `role` cell is <roleid, ftid, position, player> and roleid is the fact type joined to the position by a dot -- measured off the base, 638 rows, all width 4. A reading gives <ftid, players, ...>, so the positions are theta:iota over the players' length and the pairing is theta:zip; distl carries the ftid into each row. Nothing here is invented: the shape was read from the store the hosts used to write.",
DEF("compile:roles",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S2(A("ALPHA"),
          S5(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"),
                S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")),
                   S4(A("CONS"), N(1), K(A(".")), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(2))))),
             N(1),
             S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(2)),
             S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(2)))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("distl"),
          S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:zip"),
                S3(A("CONS"),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:iota"), S3(A("COMP"), A("length"), N(2))),
                   N(2))))))),

DEF("compile:model",
    S3(A("CONS"), N(2), A("compile:statements"))),

"WHICH FACT TYPE ANSWERS AUTHORIZATION -- A DESIGNATION, NOT A SCHEMA. Canon must not hardcode apps/access's own fact type names: RBAC is one rule shape and ownership, tenancy and time-boxed grants are the same hook with a different rule, all of them derived populations reached by the same lfp, none of them a policy engine. So canon names the CELL where an app says which fact type answers <caller, operation, resource>, and the app populates it. This is the metamodel's own binding idiom -- `Derivation Rule is provided by Constraint` at core.md:949 binds one thing to another as an instance fact -- applied to authorization.",
DEF("auth:designation",
    K(A("Authorization_is_answered_by_Fact_Type"))),

"THE EMPTY CASE IS A DECLARATION, NOT AN ACCIDENT. apps/access says an engine without it ingested proceeds ungoverned, `one closed-default declaration away`; this is that declaration. With no designation the authorized population is empty, and empty has to mean either everything or nothing -- so it is written down as canon data rather than left to whichever host asks first. CLOSED is the default because the alternative fails open: an app that forgets to designate would serve every control to every caller, and a HATEOAS surface that over-offers is worse than one that under-offers, since the client cannot tell the difference from a representation.",
DEF("auth:default_policy",
    K(A("closed"))),

DEF("auth:ft_of",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
                S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_fetch")),
                   S3(A("CONS"), A("auth:designation"), K(N(1))))),
             A("id"))))),

"FORML TEXT TO THE ROWS read:parse READS -- the step that had no implementation left. read:row's note calls its input `tokenized sentence rows <indent, token-pairs>, each token a <original, Capitalized> pair -- tokenization and case are the lex boundary externalized`, and externalized meant the fat hosts did it. They are gone, so the fleet could compile a parse and not produce one. Nothing new is needed: lex:parts ALREADY tokenizes exactly as the witnesses do, splitting `(.Nr)` into ( . Nr ) where the registered lex leaves it whole, and cn:pascalw is already the capitaliser. What was missing was only their composition. The trailing period goes because a FORML statement ends in one and the witness rows do not carry it; a sentence that does not end in punctuation would lose a token, which is a grammar the fragment does not admit. MEASURED: 57 of the reader's 59 witness rows are reproduced from reconstructed text BYTE-IDENTICALLY. The two that are not are instance facts carrying QUOTED LITERALS -- 'OSI-1' must stay one token and lex:parts splits it into ' OSI 1 ' -- so populations do not yet round-trip. That is a gap in lex:parts, not in this composition, and it is the third time this session that a quoted span not treated atomically has broken something.",
"A QUOTED LITERAL IS ONE TOKEN, which the character scan has to know while it scans. lex:punctsplit drops what it does not recognise -- 'OSI-1' comes out as ' OSI 1 ' with the hyphen GONE -- so no amount of rejoining tokens afterwards can reconstruct the literal: the information is destroyed before tokens exist. This is a SECOND scanner rather than a quote flag threaded through the first, because every reading in every app flows through lex:punctsplit and only the text-to-rows path needs this. Additive beats surgical when the blast radius is the corpus.",
DEF("lex:q_alnum",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:or"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("charisup"), K(A("T")))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:or"),
             S3(A("CONS"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("charislow"), K(A("T")))),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("charisdigit"), K(A("T"))))))))),

DEF("lex:q_flush",
    S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(2)), N(1),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))))),

"THE SCAN. State <out, run, chars, inquote>. A quote OPENS by flushing the run and starting a new one with the quote itself, and CLOSES by appending the quote and flushing -- so the delimiters stay in the token, which is what the corpus writes and what read:parse matches on. Inside a quote every character is taken verbatim, spaces and hyphens included, which is the whole point: 'Data Link' is one token and so is 'OSI-1'. Outside, it is lex:punctsplit's own rule -- alphanumerics accumulate, anything else flushes and stands alone.",
DEF("lex:qstep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)), K(A("'")))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(4), K(A("T")))),
          S5(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                      S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))))),
             K(PHI()), S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("F"))),
          S5(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_flush"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
             S2(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("T")))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(4), K(A("T")))),
          S5(A("CONS"), N(1),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("T"))),
          S4(A("COND"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_alnum"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
             S5(A("CONS"), N(1),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("F"))),
             S5(A("CONS"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                   S3(A("CONS"),
                      S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_flush"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
                      S2(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))))),
                K(PHI()), S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("F"))))))),

DEF("lex:qsplit",
    S4(A("COMP"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_flush"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
       S3(A("WHILE"), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("null"), N(3)), A("lex:qstep")),
       S5(A("CONS"), K(PHI()), K(PHI()), A("chars"), K(A("F"))))),

"A SPACE OUTSIDE A QUOTE IS A SEPARATOR, NOT A TOKEN. The scan flushes on it like any non-alphanumeric, which would leave a bare space standing as its own token; INSIDE a quote it was already taken verbatim by the inquote arm, so dropping it here cannot touch 'Data Link'. Filtered after imploding rather than special-cased in the step, so the scan keeps one rule for everything that is not alphanumeric.",
DEF("lex:qparts",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), K(A(" ")))),
                K(PHI()), S2(A("CONS"), A("id")))),
          S3(A("COMP"),
             S2(A("ALPHA"), S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")), A("id")))),
             A("lex:qsplit"))))),

DEF("read:tokens_of",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:butlast"),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S4(A("COND"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), A("chars")), K(A("'")))),
                S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("id")),
                S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("cn:pascalw")))),
          A("lex:qparts")))),

DEF("read:row_of",
    S3(A("CONS"), K(N(0)), A("read:tokens_of"))),

"THE OUTCOMES THE CREATE EQUATION DISTINGUISHES, which is the part HTTP status is actually derived FROM. AREST.tex:222: create answers <o, D'> where D' is D_r[FILE -> P''] if V has no alethic violation and D otherwise. So the state either advances or it does not, and V can be NON-EMPTY either way, because a deontic violation is reported and still commits (system:validate_modal). Three outcomes, and they come from the theorem rather than from a protocol.",
DEF("http:outcomes",
    K(S3(A("committed"), A("committed_with_violations"), A("refused")))),

"THE NUMERIC CODES ARE A CONVENTION AND ARE SAID TO BE ONE. The paper fixes almost nothing about HTTP -- one sentence, :110, `A POST /orders request creates an Order and returns its status with the actions available from it` -- and nothing at all about status codes. So this table is a DECLARATION in the sense auth:default_policy is: a decision written down where it can be read and changed, not a derivation dressed up as one. 201 for a commit that added a fact, 200 for a commit carrying reported deontic violations in the representation, 409 for an alethic refusal because the state conflicts with a declared constraint rather than the request being malformed.",
DEF("http:status",
    K(S5(S2(A("committed"), N(201)),
         S2(A("committed_with_violations"), N(200)),
         S2(A("refused"), N(409)),
         S2(A("read"), N(200)),
         S2(A("method_not_admitted"), N(405))))),

"WHICH ROUTE KIND A METHOD ADDRESSES. Theorem 2 partitions links(e) into transitions (kind i) and nav (kind ii), `disjoint by their operation kind`, and this is the HTTP spelling of that partition -- the only part of the mapping the sources determine. GET reaches a nav route because following one retrieves a peer and changes nothing; POST reaches a transition because :110 has POST creating and a fired trigger fact is what advances a machine. PUT, PATCH and DELETE have no row: the fragment admits no route they name, and an absent row refuses rather than guessing, which is Definition 5 one level up.",
DEF("http:method_kinds",
    K(S2(S2(A("GET"), A("nav")), S2(A("POST"), A("transition"))))),

"MEDIA TYPE TO RENDERER. ui:render dispatches by rho-application to render:<control>, and canon already defines render:json, render:json_atom and render:json_seq -- the JSON representation was never a host concern. One row, because one renderer exists; a second media type is a second render: DEF and a row beside this one, not a branch anywhere.",
DEF("http:media_renderers",
    K(S1(S2(A("application/json"), A("render:json"))))),

"THE STATUS FOR AN OUTCOME, refusing an outcome it does not name rather than defaulting to 200 -- a serving path that answers OK for something it did not understand is the one failure a client cannot detect.",
DEF("http:status_of",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), N(2)),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("http:status"))))),

"ONE REQUEST, ONE ANSWER: <body, status>. Operand <store, method, resource, caller, links>. This is the entry a serving host calls, and the whole of what it does is canon -- admit the method against http:method_kinds, restrict the links to what this caller may do, render the representation with render:json, and take the status from the outcome. A host that binds a socket adds no branch: it reads a request, applies this, writes the two answers. Sam: nothing custom should wrangle it into a HATEOAS server besides the native registrations required for serving. AREST.tex:260 is why that is possible -- every component of repr(e), the selectors on its facts, the derived facts, the violations and links(e), is (rho f):P for some object f, so there is nothing left for a host to compose. AN UNADMITTED METHOD ANSWERS 405 AND AN EMPTY BODY rather than an empty link set, because a client that cannot tell `you may do nothing here` from `I do not serve that verb` will retry forever.",
DEF("main:api",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(2), A("http:method_kinds")))),
       S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("http:status_of"), K(A("method_not_admitted")))),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("render:json"),
             S3(A("CONS"),
                S3(A("CONS"), K(A("resource")), N(3)),
                S3(A("CONS"), K(A("links")),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("auth:links"),
                      S5(A("CONS"), N(1), N(4), N(3), N(5)))))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("http:status_of"), K(A("read")))))),

"WHAT OPERATION A LINK OFFERS. Theorem 2 splits links(e) into two route kinds that are `disjoint by their operation kind`, and the two are told apart by ARITY in the answer itself: a nav link is <entity, peer> and a transition link is <from-status, event, to-status>. A transition's operation is its EVENT -- the label the client would follow -- and a nav link's is a read, because following one retrieves a peer and changes nothing. So the same restriction covers both without a second path.",
DEF("auth:op_of_link",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("length"), K(N(3)))),
       N(2),
       K(A("GET")))),

"LINKS(e) RESTRICTED TO WHAT THIS CALLER MAY DO -- the strengthening of Theorem 2. The theorem proves links(e) equals the OPERATIONALLY admissible controls; unrestricted, that is admissible for anyone, so a representation is sound only for an omniscient client and every agent discovers its permissions by collecting refusals. Intersecting with the authorized population makes an unauthorized control never EMITTED rather than emitted and then refused. It stays a theta-restriction over a population, so it is canon and not middleware, and it WRAPS nav:links rather than changing it: Theorem 2's machinery is untouched and this composes on top, which is what strengthening a theorem means. Operand <store, caller, entity, links>.",
DEF("auth:links",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S4(A("COND"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("auth:authorized"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)),
                      S4(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)),
                         S3(A("COMP"), A("auth:op_of_link"), N(2)),
                         S3(A("COMP"), N(3), N(1))))),
                S2(A("CONS"), N(2)),
                K(PHI()))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("distl"),
             S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2), N(3)), N(4)))))),

"IS THIS CALLER AUTHORIZED FOR THIS OPERATION ON THIS RESOURCE. Operand <store, <caller, operation, resource>>. No designation answers the default; a designation answers membership in the population it names, which is ordinary theta:member over a derived relation -- Definition Population's `membership is application` doing the work that a policy engine would otherwise do.",
DEF("auth:authorized",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), A("auth:ft_of"), N(1))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("auth:default_policy"), K(A("open")))),
          K(A("T")), K(A("F"))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
          S3(A("CONS"), N(2),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
                S3(A("CONS"),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
                      S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_fetch")),
                         S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("auth:ft_of"), N(1)), K(N(3))))),
                   N(1))))))),

"THE ENTRY THAT TAKES A SUB-KIND. compile:builder answers from the kind alone and is the degenerate case -- eight of the ten need nothing more. This is the whole map: try the direct row, then ring by computed name, then value by table, then refuse. The refusal stays the empty sequence at every arm, so an unadmitted ring kind and an unknown value shape are as visibly not-here as an unadmitted constraint kind, which is Definition 5 holding one level down.",
DEF("compile:builder_of",
    S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:builder"), N(1))),
       S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
                        S3(A("CONS"), N(1), A("compile:ring_tags"))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:ring_builder"), N(1)),
          S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), K(A("value")))),
             S3(A("COMP"),
                S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), N(2)),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), N(2), A("compile:value_builders")))),
             K(PHI()))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:builder"), N(1)))),

"THE TWO KINDS DEFINITION 4 LISTS AND CANON CANNOT YET BUILD. cardinality has no constraints: object at all, and value_comparison has none either -- its lt/gt/le/ge are filter operators rather than a constraint builder. Written as data because a gap discovered only when a reading fails is a gap nothing can check for. compile: is not complete against Definition 4 until these two have objects, and this is the list of what is owed.",
"NOTHING IS OWED HERE ANY MORE. This row listed the kinds Definition 4 names and canon could not build; both have since been built (constraints:value_comparison, constraints:cardinality), so it is empty. It is kept rather than deleted because an empty list is the checkable claim -- a reader can ask canon whether anything is owed and get an answer, where a deleted DEF only means nobody wrote one.",
DEF("compile:builders_absent",
    K(PHI())),

"THE LOOKUP THAT MAKES THE TABLE THE DISPATCH. Without this the rows only DESCRIBE a dispatch that something else performs, which is how the map came to be host control flow in the first place. theta:find_desc already is the lookup -- distl the key over the rows, fold with theta:keep_named, take the first match -- so the whole of compile:builder is the miss case: a kind with no direct row answers the empty sequence, not a default. That is Definition 5 again, and it is the same refusal compile:family gives, so a fanned kind (ring, value) and an absent one (cardinality, value_comparison) are both simply not here yet rather than silently mapped to something plausible.",
DEF("compile:builder",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:builders_direct")))),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), N(2)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:builders_variant")))),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), N(2)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("compile:builders_direct")))))),

"THE VALUE COMPARISON, which Definition 4 lists and canon could not build. AREST.tex:148 places it in the membership family with one substitution -- the comparison constraints take the INEQUALITY theta where the others take equality -- so it is theta:Filter over a predicate that applies the named operator to two selected roles, negated, because a constraint answers the rows that VIOLATE it. Operand <op, i, j>: the operator atom from system:cmp_ops (lt gt le ge eq, and its `is before`/`is after` spellings) and the two role positions. lt gt le ge are base primitives, so nothing new is registered. This is the one I earlier recorded as already met: the SENTENCE parses and flags exactly the violating row through the filter operators, which is why it read as done, but at the level of a constraint OBJECT there was nothing here.",
DEF("constraints:value_comparison",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:Filter"),
       S4(A("CONS"), K(A("COMP")), K(A("not")),
          S4(A("CONS"), K(A("COMP")), N(1),
             S4(A("CONS"), K(A("CONS")), N(2), N(3)))))),

"THE CARDINALITY BOUNDS, one each. metamodel/core.md:769 declares the kind -- Cardinality Constraint has Min Occurrence, has Max Occurrence, at most one of each -- and its audit note fixes the semantics against the neighbour it is confused with: a bound on the SIZE of a type's population, distinct from frequency's per-value occurrence bound. So the count is `length` over the whole population where frequency counts matches per key, and these two are the count-against-a-declared-bound halves the paper's first family names.",
DEF("constraints:card_lo",
    S4(A("CONS"), K(A("COMP")), K(A("lt")),
       S4(A("CONS"), K(A("CONS")), K(A("length")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("CONST")), N(1))))),

DEF("constraints:card_hi",
    S4(A("CONS"), K(A("COMP")), K(A("gt")),
       S4(A("CONS"), K(A("CONS")), K(A("length")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("CONST")), N(2))))),

"THE CARDINALITY CONSTRAINT. Operand <min, max>, either absent, because the metamodel makes both at-most-one. A VIOLATION HERE IS NOT A ROW: the population is too small or too large as a whole, so the object answers the WHOLE population when it is out of bounds and the empty sequence when it is not -- which keeps Definition Violation's contract, where a non-empty answer is the violation, without pretending some particular row is at fault. Both bounds absent can never be violated, matching constraints:frequency's own null cascade rather than inventing a second convention for the same situation.",
DEF("constraints:cardinality",
    S5(A("CONS"), K(A("COND")),
       S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(1)),
          S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(2)),
             K(S2(A("CONST"), A("F"))),
             A("constraints:card_hi")),
          S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(2)),
             A("constraints:card_lo"),
             S4(A("CONS"), K(A("COMP")), K(A("fp:or2")),
                S4(A("CONS"), K(A("CONS")), A("constraints:card_lo"), A("constraints:card_hi"))))),
       K(A("id")),
       K(S2(A("CONST"), PHI()))))
)
